WEBVTT

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.630
Welcome to NASA Launchpad. I'm your host, Audrey Staples.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.880
As we've mentioned before on Launchpad, NASA doesn't just do work in space.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.980
The vast majority of NASA's workforce is right here.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.400
on earth and there's a lot of work going on to understand our own planet our planet is made up

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.510
of unique communities known as biomes how do you determine what makes up a biome well for the answer

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:47.650
let's head out to arizona and check out the amazing structure that is biosphere 2. bay 2 as it's

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:53.350
known is currently operated for environmental research by the university of arizona so let's

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:59.450
hear about biomes from matt adamson biosphere 2's program coordinator for education and outreach so

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.480
A biome is a major geographical region on Earth that contains its own unique or distinctive

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.710
sets of plant and animal life.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:13.250
So you have things like savannas, oceans, deserts, tropical rainforests.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.390
All of these would be considered major geographic regions with their own unique plant and animal

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:17.590
life.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:21.870
Inside Biosphere 2, the biomes we have are all either tropical or subtropical in nature,

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.620
and so we have things like a tropical rainforest, a savanna, which is a grassland, a coastal

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.940
fog desert, and we do have our own saltwater ocean.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.180
Ecologists use patterns of climate, looking at factors like temperature and precipitation,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.780
as well as the kinds of plants or animals that live in an area to divide the world into

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.620
different biomes.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:46.990
And although every kind of biome on Earth is not represented in this amazing structure,

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:53.310
each biome and Biosphere 2 is based on an actual biome here on Earth, complete with smaller

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:53.990
ecosystems.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.420
And the biomes inside Biosphere 2 make it an ideal place for research.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:01:59.960
But what?

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.810
exactly is being studied? I'll let Matt explain. There is a fundamental question

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:10.470
about that connects terrestrial ecology to global climate change and we want to

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:15.870
study the sensitivity of key terrestrial biomes as they relate to variability in

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.710
global climate change. By running both drought and warming experiments in this

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:25.340
controlled ecosystem we expect to gain a more mechanistic understanding of the

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.510
future response of tropical rainforests to climate change from the individual

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:29.850
plant.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.470
level all the way up to the level of an entire ecosystem.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:37.270
Okay, we know that all living things are closely related to their environment.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.300
Any change in one part of that environment can cause a ripple effect throughout the

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.020
other parts.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:46.070
And B2 uses biomes in the world to learn about those ripples.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:51.580
But why rainforest, especially the largest one in the world, the Amazon rainforest in

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.300
South America?

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:58.410
So the Amazon is of interest because it's one of these key sensitive terrestrial biomes

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.750
I mentioned, in that it contains

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.160
unparalleled biological diversity, it contains a globally significant store of

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.190
organic carbon, and it is a potent engine that drives the water and climate cycle

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:15.300
on Earth. I understand why the University of Arizona wants to learn more about

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.830
biological diversity or the variety of life found here on Earth, but how does NASA

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:24.720
fit into all of this? So our significant rainforest research really is connected

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.500
to two key legacies of the large-scale biosphere atmosphere experiment in

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:29.740
Amazonia.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.370
that was funded in large part by NASA.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:38.640
So essentially what that means is we're taking data that NASA has already paid for through other projects

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.070
and using it to compare the responses in our rainforest here, which is in a controlled setting,

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:52.190
so that when we're changing things like gases in the atmosphere, temperature, reducing precipitation when we put them through droughts,

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:59.860
we then can compare this sort of controlled data to real-world data that NASA's collected through these, for example, these floodgates.

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.700
that are collecting data throughout the Amazon.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.740
So sort of a synthesis of real-world data collection

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.300
and controlled world data collection.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.030
Talk about having a great setup to change an independent variable,

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.600
observe the results, or dependent variable,

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:14.880
and compare those...

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.500
results to real-world data.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.560
I guess those aren't just words in your textbook.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.030
Now, what about a specific example of this research?

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.160
One that is underway and has been for some time

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.900
is a study of volatile organic compounds

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.580
in the atmosphere of the rainforest.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.120
And volatile organic compounds essentially are the 1% of our atmosphere.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:39.600
If you consider that 78% of our atmosphere is nitrogen, 21% is oxygen,

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.980
the remaining 1% or less are things that we would think of as greenhouse gases, carbon dioxide.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.460
dioxide, ethanol, things like that.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.780
And the person who's studying VOCs in our rainforest

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.950
is particularly interested in the natural production

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.790
of ethanol in rainforest systems.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.310
So we're actually collecting gas samples

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.980
from the rainforest and then...

00:05:00.028 --> 00:05:02.028
analyzing them in his lab.

00:05:02.076 --> 00:05:05.516
He hopes to get a baseline understanding of natural production of ethanol.

00:05:06.044 --> 00:05:08.044
because if we think about

00:05:08.348 --> 00:05:11.728
Moving to ethanol as an alternative energy source so as we power

00:05:12.348 --> 00:05:14.588
Automobiles, for example, with ethanol instead of gasoline.

00:05:15.196 --> 00:05:19.516
Well, it could be that we're substituting one greenhouse gas, CO2.

00:05:19.964 --> 00:05:21.764
for another in ethanol.

00:05:22.364 --> 00:05:24.804
So he wants to understand some of the natural occurring.

00:05:25.052 --> 00:05:27.052
ethanol before we as

00:05:27.072 --> 00:05:29.972
human beings perhaps start putting a lot of industrial produced ethanol.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.110
into the atmosphere. So that's one example of how we're using our rainforest to study

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:40.360
natural processes and in this case an analog to the Amazon. It just goes to show you the

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:44.560
scientific process is never complete. Scientists are continually making observations.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:49.210
collecting data, predicting results, and analyzing their findings.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:51.510
That's it for now. I'm Audrey Staples.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:54.630
Thanks for watching, and we'll catch you next time on NASA Launch Pad.
